<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Neopatras</h1><p class="st-meta">1319 CE – 1390 CE (approx.) · capital Neopatras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Catalan duchy in southern Thessaly, created on the ruins of the Greek Principality of Thessaly and ruled with Athens by the kings of Sicily and Aragon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-athens">Duchy of Athens</a> in 1311, it pushed north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-doukas">John II Doukas</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-thessaly">Principality of Thessaly</a> died without heirs in 1318, the Catalans occupied the south of the region with its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a> (modern Ypati) and organised it as a duchy in 1319.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a> was held together with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> by the Aragonese kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, who appointed vicars-general such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-fadrique">Alfonso Fadrique</a> to govern it. A small Catalan military class ruled over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a>, following the customs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. In 1379-1380 the duchies passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a>, who called the Acropolis the most beautiful jewel in the world.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navarrese-company">Navarrese Company</a>, the Florentine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerio-i-acciaioli">Nerio Acciaioli</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> broke the Catalan hold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a> fell around 1390, and the Ottomans took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> in the 1390s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-neopatras.jpg" alt="Duchy of Neopatras" width="320" height="339"><figcaption>Image: Rowanwindwhistler (Original author) Indpcatll (Translation) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ducat_de_Neop%C3%A0tria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1319 CE – 1390 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Neopatras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alfonso Fadrique, Peter IV of Aragon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Catalan, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Neopatria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1354&amp;focus=duchy-of-neopatras" aria-label="Duchy of Neopatras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5234 790 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Neopatras lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5554 1046L5580 1057M5554 1046L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5580 1057h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5554 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5554 1046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1354&amp;focus=duchy-of-neopatras">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

